  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#navigate step 12 in the javascript: case, substep 1 does a check that involves the \"source browsing context's active document's origin\".\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, this need not match the origin that browsing context had when the navigation _started_, because this step is running off a task.  So in a browser that implements the spec as written you get a security hole: if you target a javascript: load at a cross-origin site just as you're being unloaded due to navigation to that same cross-origin site you get XSS.\r\n\r\nI know there are existing bugs on the \"source browsing context\" thing being a bit wonky, but given that I know there are implementations attempting to align with the HTML spec on the navigation algorithm, or even implementing directly from the spec, it would be good to at least have a note here or something explicitly calling out the fact that the spec should not be implemented as-is.\r\n\r\n// cc @asajeffrey @cbrewster ",
